{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The mechanism states that after a crash an external auditor must 'recompute the workspace from the prepared root' to verify emptiness, but a Merkle root is a one-way digest that cannot be used to reconstruct workspace contents or to inspect individual nodes for evidence. Because the protocol durably records only this root and a post-hoc attestation, it provides no causal means to perform the audit it requires: once the volatile workspace is lost, the auditor lacks the data needed to check for evidence, so the protocol cannot guarantee that reclamation will never silently displace completed evidence.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"The protocol asserts silent displacement is structurally impossible because evidence is content-addressed, but immutability of individual nodes does not causally prevent displacement: the target itself notes that the Merkle-DAG root pointer or naming index may still be mutable. The mechanism provides no durable record of evidence roots or workspace ancestry to anchor that index after a crash, so reclamation has no causal foothold to know which nodes are still reachable evidence and which are orphans. A silent update to the root pointer can therefore exclude completed evidence without altering the immutable nodes, violating the guarantee.","counterexample":null}]}